WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.060
Can a steady signal fail where pulses work?

2
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.680
Gonadotropin-releasing hormone, GnRH, is a peptide from the hypothalamus.

3
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:19.300
It tells the pituitary to release the gonadotropins, which drive the ovaries and

4
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:26.260
testes. In 1978, Belchetz and colleagues studied rhesus monkeys whose hypothalamic

5
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.680
lesions had abolished gonadotropin release.

6
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:33.713
They gave GnRH continuously, or once an hour.

7
00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:37.473
Hourly pulses restored gonadotropin secretion.

8
00:00:38.153 --> 00:00:40.113
A constant infusion did not.

9
00:00:41.193 --> 00:00:46.933
Clinics use the same effect on purpose, keeping a GnRH agonist present to shut

10
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:48.513
gonadotropin output down.

11
00:00:49.317 --> 00:00:56.397
One general possibility is that off switches build up while a signal is on, and need

12
00:00:56.397 --> 00:01:00.037
a gap to reverse. Pulses give that gap.

13
00:01:01.117 --> 00:01:05.577
But a kinase mark and an arrestin cannot be the whole explanation.

14
00:01:07.037 --> 00:01:14.057
Willars and colleagues showed in 1999 that the mammalian GnRH receptor has no

15
00:01:14.057 --> 00:01:20.997
C-terminal tail. So it resists phosphorylation by agonist, and resists rapid desensitization.

16
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.510
A failure at the receptor would show as fewer receptors on the surface, or less

17
00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.290
second messenger made per dose.

18
00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:35.470
A failure downstream would leave the receptor's own output normal, while hormone

19
00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:40.790
release falls. Measure second messenger output, and you can tell the two apart.

20
00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.270
Published work answers only part of this question.
